# core.autocrlf is true on the Windows machine, so text files land in the
# working tree with CRLF. For shell scripts that is not cosmetic: they are run
# by WSL's bash, where the CR becomes part of the token and `set -euo pipefail`
# dies with "set: pipefail: invalid option name" on line 12 of windows/sync.sh.
# Pin them to LF so the same checkout works from both sides.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning, different owner: nothing but sync.sh ever writes the GlazeWM
# config — the WM only reads it — so both sides can be held at LF, which is how
# the file was authored and how the running copy looks. Without this the CRLF
# checkout would make the pair report DIFFERS until the next push.
windows/glazewm/config.yaml text eol=lf

# Deliberately NOT `* text=auto eol=lf`. The rest of windows/sync.sh's PAIRS are
# compared byte-for-byte (`diff -q`) against copies owned by Windows apps, which
# rewrite them with CRLF whenever you edit through their UI. A LF working tree
# would therefore make those pairs report DIFFERS on every run, turning the one
# signal that flags real drift into constant noise. They keep the platform
# default on purpose.
